      "math_academy_id": "q-42259",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law",
      "problem": "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
